@startuml fig2_functional_diagram
' Diagram 2 of the IEC 60050-351 pattern: the functional diagram.
'
' Conventions: shared/FIGURES.md. Flat boxes, no namespace prefixes, no clause
' numbers, orthogonal edges, nothing explanatory inside the drawing.
'
' What it shows: how an action path is resolved into the chain of functional
' units it runs through, after Figures 1 and 2 of the standard, and how a
' functional unit relates to the hardware realising it.
'
' Left out on purpose:
'  - the kinds of control and the action: diagram 1
'  - functions and characteristics: diagram 3
'  - the variable taxonomy below VariableQuantity, which would double the box
'    count without adding to the structure the diagram is about
'  - the twelve subclasses of FunctionalUnit that Figures 1 and 2 name: those
'    are diagram 2b, since twelve boxes on one node crowd out the mechanism
'    this diagram is about
'
' Read left to right: the path decomposes into lines, a line runs between two
' functional units, and a functional unit is realised by physical units.

skinparam backgroundColor transparent
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Arial
skinparam defaultFontSize 14
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam nodesep 25
skinparam ranksep 45
skinparam padding 3
skinparam ArrowColor #1A2733
skinparam ArrowFontSize 13
skinparam ArrowFontColor #1A2733
hide empty members
hide circle
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor #FCEFC7
  BorderColor #C9A227
  FontColor #1A2733
  FontSize 14
  BorderThickness 1
}

' ===================================================================
'  The path decomposes into lines
' ===================================================================

class ActionPath
class ActionLine
class ForwardPath
class FeedbackPath
class ControlLoop

ActionPath <|-- ForwardPath
ActionPath <|-- FeedbackPath

ActionPath  --> "1..*" ActionLine   : hasActionLine
ControlLoop --> "1"    ForwardPath  : hasForwardPath
ControlLoop --> "1"    FeedbackPath : hasFeedbackPath

' ===================================================================
'  A line runs between two units and carries one variable
' ===================================================================

class FunctionalUnit
class VariableQuantity

' Two parallel edges to the same class. Given the same direction they are drawn
' on top of each other and their labels collide, so the second is routed the
' other way round. It therefore reads as isTargetOfActionLine, the inverse the
' pattern declares for toUnit: the same statement seen from the unit.
ActionLine     -down->  "1" FunctionalUnit   : fromUnit
FunctionalUnit -up->    "*" ActionLine       : isTargetOfActionLine
ActionLine     -left->  "1" VariableQuantity : carriesVariable

' ===================================================================
'  The two views of an item under consideration
' ===================================================================

class ItemUnderConsideration
class PhysicalUnit

ItemUnderConsideration <|-- FunctionalUnit
ItemUnderConsideration <|-- PhysicalUnit

' ===================================================================
'  Composition and realisation
' ===================================================================

FunctionalUnit --> "*" FunctionalUnit : consistsOfUnit
FunctionalUnit --> "*" PhysicalUnit   : realisedByPhysicalUnit

@enduml
